 "title": "Жажда веселья  (Thirst for Fun)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "sasha": "Саша"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "At the restaurant, Max finds Sasha admiring a new cake she has decorated. He compliments her, and he has an idea for what to do next. Listen to it before we take it apart.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Саша, ты видела новый красивый торт?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Конечно, я сама его сегодня украшала.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Он выглядит просто великолепно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Спасибо, я очень сильно старалась.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф уже видел твою новую работу?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да, он был очень доволен мной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "И это у тебя получилось.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "А теперь можно немного отдохнуть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Кто тут хочет повеселиться?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Я хочу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Тогда пойдём со мной на улицу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Подожди, я только сниму свой фартук.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Their chat again, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Саша, ты видела новый красивый торт?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Sasha, did you see the new beautiful cake?' Now let's inspect the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "видела",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has a feminine ending since the question is to a woman. The cake, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "торт",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", is masculine, so ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "новый",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "красивый",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " match it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Конечно, я сама его сегодня украшала.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Of course, I decorated it myself today.' We will focus on the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "сама",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has a feminine ending. And the cake is masculine again, so 'it' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "его",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'him'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Он выглядит просто великолепно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It looks simply magnificent.' The language says ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Он",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "выглядит",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'he looks', about the cake. This is followed by ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "великолепно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " where English uses 'magnificent'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Спасибо, я очень сильно старалась.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you, I tried very hard.' We will focus on the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "старалась",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'tried hard', with a shortened ending after a vowel. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "очень",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "сильно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " reads as 'very strongly'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф уже видел твою новую работу?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Has the chef already seen your new work?' The work, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "работу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", changes ending as the thing seen, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "твою",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "новую",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " take matching endings.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да, он был очень доволен мной.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, he was very satisfied with me.' After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "доволен",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'pleased', the person takes a 'with' form, so 'I' becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "мной",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". The word for pleased has no ending because the chef is a man.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "И это у тебя получилось.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And you pulled it off.' This reads as 'and this at you turned out'. People say ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "у",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "тебя",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "получилось",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " for 'you managed it': the success happens 'at you' rather than being done by you.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "А теперь можно немного отдохнуть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And now we can rest a little.' We will look at the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "можно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'it is allowed' or 'one can', and it works without any 'we' or 'I'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "отдохнуть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'to have a rest', follows it in the basic form.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Кто тут хочет повеселиться?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who wants to have some fun?' Let's focus on this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "повеселиться",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built on a word for cheerful, with pieces added to mean 'enjoy oneself for a bit'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Я хочу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I do.' The answer is just ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Я",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "хочу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'I want'. It is common to answer a question by repeating its verb instead of saying 'yes'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Тогда пойдём со мной на улицу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then let us go outside with me.' Outside here is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "улицу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", reading as 'onto the street'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "пойдём",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'let us go', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "со",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "мной",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'with me'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Подожди, я только сниму свой фартук.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Wait, I will just take off my apron.' Let us consider the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "сниму",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'I will take off', with no 'will' word needed. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "свой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "фартук",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " uses a word meaning 'one's own' instead of 'my'. Now the whole chat, straight through.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
